          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Asthma with an acute exacerbation: is that one code, or a code plus something else?",
          "answer": "The retrieved coding guidance does not give the full answer to whether this is one code or a code plus another. The asthma document (coding-asthma-j45) says asthma coding usually comes down to two lookups, one for severity and one for exacerbation. It does not list the specific code values in the text I received.\n\nThe same document says exacerbation and status asthmaticus are alternatives, not stacked. If the record documents status asthmaticus, the code says that and does not also say acute exacerbation. It also says that when severity is not documented, the unspecified asthma code is the correct choice.\n\nThe chapter overview (coding-icd10-chapter-10-respiratory) places asthma in the J40 to J47 block of chronic lower respiratory diseases. The COPD document (coding-copd-j44) says that when asthma appears alongside COPD, an extra code identifying the type of asthma is added. I did not find guidance on whether an exacerbation code is ever paired with another asthma code.\n\nFor the exact code and any add-on codes, check the current official ICD-10-CM tables or ask a certified medical coder. Coding decisions depend on what the clinical record documents.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
